@page "/vek/introduction"

<DocsPage DisplayFooter="true">
    <DocsPageHeader Title="What is Vek?" SubTitle="Vek is a component library for building consistent, capable Blazor interfaces, supported by an open-source community and a growing ecosystem." />
    <DocsPageContent>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Build interfaces from ready-made parts">
                <Description>
                    <VekText>A polished interface takes time: each control needs clear behavior, useful states, and a place in the larger design. Vek gives Blazor teams a library of reusable building blocks, so everyday UI work does not have to start from zero.</VekText>
                    <VekText>Use components for common tasks such as actions, forms, navigation, menus, dialogs, tabs, and data grids. Compose them in Razor, connect them to your app state, and keep working with the Blazor patterns you already know.</VekText>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="A consistent foundation that stays flexible">
                <Description>
                    <VekText>Vek takes inspiration from Material Design and gives teams room to make the interface their own. Shared theme settings and CSS variables help keep colors, spacing, and other visual choices aligned across an application.</VekText>
                    <VekText>Choose from established component variants, then customize the details your product needs. Vek supports light and dark themes, and components can adapt to right-to-left layouts for languages that read in that direction.</VekText>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Ready for real application workflows">
                <Description>
                    <VekText>Applications need more than buttons and labels. Vek includes the building blocks for forms, validation, date selection, tables, charts, overlays, and responsive page layouts, so related parts of an interface can work together.</VekText>
                    <VekText>Keyboard behavior, accessible states, and layout direction are part of component design. The documentation pairs API details with practical examples to help you understand a component before bringing it into your app.</VekText>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="An open project shaped by its community">
                <Description>
                    <VekText>Vek is developed in the open. Explore the source, documentation, and tests, then tailor the library's components and theming tools to the needs of your project.</VekText>
                    <VekText>Use Vek in your own work, share an issue when something can improve, or contribute code, examples, and documentation. Each contribution helps make the library more useful for the next Blazor team.</VekText>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Start with one component">
                <Description>
                    <VekText>Pick a small piece of your interface and explore the matching component page. For example, a filled <CodeInline>VekButton</CodeInline> can give an important action a consistent style and interaction:</VekText>
                    <VekText><code>&lt;VekButton Color="Color.Primary" Variant="Variant.Filled"&gt;Save changes&lt;/VekButton&gt;</code></VekText>
                    <VekText>Browse the component examples, adjust the theme to fit your application, and build from there. When you are ready to take part, visit the <VekLink Href="https://github.com/madiseh/Vek">Vek project on GitHub</VekLink>.</VekText>
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
        </DocsPageSection>

    </DocsPageContent>
</DocsPage>
